---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyTimeCard from '@/components/tools/datetime/CalcMoneyTimeCard.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="time-card-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyTimeCard client:load variant="week" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Filling in a time card fast</h2>
    <p>
      The time fields accept many formats. <code>9</code>, <code>9:00</code>, <code>0900</code>,
      <code>9am</code> and <code>09:00</code> all mean nine in the morning; <code>530p</code>,
      <code>17:30</code> and <code>5:30 pm</code> all mean half past five in the afternoon. There is no
      dropdown or required separator, and no validation runs while you type.
    </p>
    <p>
      A blank day adds nothing. Enter the unpaid break in minutes; leave paid breaks at zero, because they are
      part of the shift.
    </p>

    <h2>Overnight shifts</h2>
    <p>
      If the out time is at or before the in time, the calculator treats the shift as crossing midnight and adds
      twenty-four hours. A shift from 22:00 to 06:00 is eight hours, and the row has a label that shows this.
      This covers nights, split hospitality shifts and any shift past twelve.
    </p>

    <h2>The two overtime rules</h2>
    <p>
      Overtime law varies widely, so the daily and weekly rules are separate switches.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Where</th>
            <th>Rule</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>US federal (FLSA)</td>
            <td>Time and a half above 40 hours in a workweek, for non-exempt employees. No daily rule.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>California</td>
            <td>Time and a half above 8 hours a day and above 40 a week; double time above 12 a day.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Canada (most provinces)</td>
            <td>Time and a half above 8 a day or 40–44 a week, depending on the province.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>UK and much of the EU</td>
            <td>No statutory overtime premium. Rates are contractual; working-time limits apply instead.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When both switches are on, the calculator applies daily overtime first and excludes those hours before
      the weekly threshold, so no hour is paid at the premium rate twice. That double count is the most
      common error in home-made timesheet spreadsheets.
    </p>

    <h2>Decimal hours for payroll</h2>
    <p>
      Each row shows <code>h:mm</code> and the decimal value. Payroll needs the decimal form, because pay is
      hours times a rate, and <code>8:45 × 22</code> is not an arithmetic expression. Eight hours forty-five
      minutes is 8.75, not 8.45. That mistake costs eighteen minutes per shift, which over a year is more than
      a week's pay.
    </p>

    <h2>Payroll rounding</h2>
    <p>
      The rounding control rounds each entry before the total, as time-clock systems do. Quarter-hour rounding
      is the most common convention in the US, and tenth-of-an-hour in professional services.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rounding must be neutral. Under the FLSA an employer may round to the nearest increment in both
      directions, but a policy that always rounds down, or rounds clock-ins forward and clock-outs back, is
      unlawful wage theft. This calculator always rounds to the nearest increment.
    </p>

    <h2>Print it, sign it, file it</h2>
    <p>
      The print button makes a clean card without the editing controls, ready for a supervisor's signature. If
      your payroll system needs a file, export the same data to CSV, Excel or PDF. Your browser makes these
      files, so a timesheet with your name and hours does not go through another server.
    </p>

    <h2>Saving your week</h2>
    <p>
      Saving is off by default, because a timesheet contains your name, your employer and your movements. Turn
      the switch on and the week is written to this browser's local storage, on this device only, so a
      refresh does not clear it. The delete button next to the switch removes it at once. The rest of the page
      works with the switch on or off.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No tax: gross pay only. Income tax, national insurance, pension and other deductions depend on
        jurisdiction and personal circumstances.
      </li>
      <li>
        No shift differentials: night, weekend and holiday premiums vary by contract. Enter those hours as a
        separate week or adjust the rate.
      </li>
      <li>
        No accruals: holiday accrual, sick pay and time-off banks are payroll-system features, not timesheet
        arithmetic.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
